                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_ramadan:       `<strong>Right read.</strong> The Ramadan and wedding occasions are exactly where the rival's value tier is taking share — and they're the year's biggest hair-care moments. Acting on that occasion is the local engine doing its job: sensing where demand actually is, not where the global plan assumed it would be.`,
                    coach_r1_format:        `<strong>A format or fragrance shift is real, but it's not where you're bleeding.</strong> The share loss is concentrated in the Ramadan/wedding occasion at the value tier. Market orientation means acting on the signal that's costing you points, not the most interesting one.`,
                    coach_r1_priceonly:     `<strong>Matching price everywhere isn't market orientation — it's surrender.</strong> A blanket price match races your margin to the bottom and still doesn't answer the occasion the rival is winning on. Read the demand, don't just chase the discount.`,
                    coach_r1_ignore:        `<strong>That's how a subsidiary forfeits the local engine.</strong> ‘Trust the global platform’ is exactly the blind-compliance trap — the global creative was built for 40 countries and shows the wrong season for Türkiye. Sense the local signal or cede the quarter.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named the occasion the rival is taking AND the biggest global/local mismatch — the standardized creative shows the wrong season for Türkiye. That is market orientation done well, and it tells you exactly where local adaptation has to do its work.`,
                    coach_r1_nomismatch:    `<strong>There is a real mismatch — denying it is the blind-compliance trap.</strong> The global creative was built for 40 markets and shows the wrong occasion for a Turkish Ramadan/wedding season. Saying ‘it fits as-is’ is how you cede the window to the rival.`,
                    coach_r1_lowori:        `<strong>Thin market sensing leaves you flying blind.</strong> With low orientation effort you'll allocate the ₺240M on HQ's assumptions, not on where Turkish demand and the rival actually are. The local engine only works if you actually read the market.`,
                    coach_r2_dualduty:      `<strong>That is the twin-engine move.</strong> Adapting the standardized asset for the Ramadan/wedding occasion means one campaign carries both engines — it defends share AND stays on-brand, so a single lira buys local relevance and global consistency at once. That's the complementarity the research is about.`,
                    coach_r2_value:         `<strong>A separate value-tier line defends share, but it fragments the brand.</strong> You're treating global and local as two campaigns instead of one — share holds, but consistency takes a hit and you've spent twice. Could one adapted asset have done both jobs?`,
                    coach_r2_trade:         `<strong>Deep trade promotions buy a share bump you can't afford.</strong> Under the weak lira your margins are already compressed; pouring the local budget into discounts torches ROI for a share gain that evaporates when the promotion ends. Build brand, not just price.`,
                    coach_r2_hold:          `<strong>Maximal compliance cedes the Ramadan window.</strong> Holding the local budget back to look obedient to HQ hands the year's biggest occasion to the rival. ‘We followed the rule’ is not a performance story at the September council — it's an excuse.`,
                    coach_r2_caseup:        `<strong>That's the right way to deviate.</strong> Going under the 70% mandate with a defensible, transparent business case lets HQ flex the number with you rather than discover it from the tracker. You protect the relationship and the autonomy even as you bend the rule.`,
                    coach_r2_incoherent:    `<strong>Your numbers and your story don't match.</strong> You set global weight below the 70% mandate but told HQ you're not deviating. The consistency tracker will say otherwise — decide whether you're complying or negotiating, and be straight about it.`,
                    coach_r2_silent:        `<strong>Quiet deviation is the credibility killer.</strong> Running more local weight and saying nothing means HQ learns it from the consistency tracker, not from you. Even a great share result can't buy back the trust — and your case for more autonomy just got harder.`,
                    coach_r3_dualfast:      `<strong>Speed with fit — exactly the agility your award celebrated.</strong> A fast Ramadan adaptation of the global asset plus a targeted value-tier counter answers the rival in-season without abandoning the brand. You owned the window instead of watching the rival take it.`,
                    coach_r3_pricewar:      `<strong>A broad price war is the macro-blind reflex.</strong> The budget is frozen and the lira pushed your costs up ~15%, so matching the cut across the board burns margin you don't have for a share gain that won't survive the season. Defend on occasion and fit, not on price alone.`,
                    coach_r3_standpat:      `<strong>Standing pat hands the rival the season.</strong> ‘The mandate says don't move’ is blind compliance under fire — the in-season window is exactly when responsiveness matters most. A perfectly compliant plan that misses Ramadan is a loss, not a defence.`,
                    coach_r3_perfect:       `<strong>A perfect plan after the window closes is worth less than a good-enough one in week three.</strong> Ramadan media inventory is sold out and the wedding displays are the rival's. Your award was for speed — agility prizes timely action over delayed perfection.`,
                    coach_r3_slow:          `<strong>You moved too slowly.</strong> A great plan delivered late forfeits the very window it was meant to win. In-season, a faster 80%-right response beats a slower 100%-right one — that is what marketing agility means.`,
                    coach_r3_margin:        `<strong>Fast, on-brand AND margin-aware.</strong> You answered the rival in-season with an adapted asset and re-ran the ROI in today's lira, so you're only funding what still pays back. That's agility with discipline — the twin engines firing together under a macro shock.`,
                    coach_r3_macroblind:    `<strong>Your ROI is last year's ROI.</strong> The lira moved and imported inputs are up ~15%, so the activities you just funded may barely break even. Re-run the math in today's lira — which promotions actually survive the new cost base?`,
                    coach_r3_defensive:     `<strong>Insisting the tracker is wrong burns goodwill.</strong> HQ asked a fair question about a real trend; stonewalling reads as evasive and invites tighter control, not more autonomy. Show them the in-season case instead.`,
                    coach_r3_hqignore:      `<strong>Silence with HQ during a live query is the opaque-deviation trap.</strong> Leaving the consistency question unanswered means HQ fills the gap with suspicion — and they find the deviation from the tracker, not from you. That costs the subsidiary credibility.`,
                    coach_r4_complementary: `<strong>That's the pitch the research demands.</strong> Showing the standardized asset that carried local relevance proves the engines are complementary, not a seesaw — you held consistency AND defended share with the same investment. That's the agility story the council wants.`,
                    coach_r4_zerosum:       `<strong>The seesaw framing undersells your own work.</strong> If you tell the council you traded consistency for share point-for-point, you're conceding the engines are opposites — when the win was finding where one asset served both. Reframe it as complementarity.`,
                    coach_r4_compliance:    `<strong>‘We followed the rule’ is not an agility story.</strong> Pure-compliance framing forfeits the local engine in the telling — the council celebrates subsidiaries that turned the mandate into market wins, not ones that simply obeyed it.`,
                    coach_r4_shareonly:     `<strong>A share-only scorecard hides half the picture.</strong> Tracking only share lets consistency and ROI drift unseen and reads to HQ as if you don't care about the brand. Commit to all three so the agility case is defensible.`,
                    coach_r4_noscore:       `<strong>No scorecard is how the gains decay.</strong> ‘The season's over, move on’ is exactly how last year's wins evaporated. Without in-season tracking and named owners, the plan you just locked slides back the moment attention shifts.`,
                    coach_r4_earn:          `<strong>That's how a subsidiary earns latitude.</strong> Showing consistency stayed above the floor while agility delivered the season makes the business case for more local autonomy — you proved you can be trusted to run your own market.`,
                    coach_r4_grab:          `<strong>Demanding HQ drop standardization overplays your hand.</strong> The global engine captures real scale and coherence; asking to kill it reads as rogue and risks the consistency review you just avoided. Earn latitude with results, don't grab it.`,
                    coach_r4_defer:         `<strong>Deferring on everything surrenders the local engine.</strong> Total deference keeps the peace but tells HQ Türkiye has no point of view — and the next mandate will be even tighter. Agility means defending the local case, not abandoning it.`,

